---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const b = appBySlug('brevo')!;
const path = '/compare/mailchimp-vs-brevo';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-brevo');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-brevo');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is the current Shopify integration just a contact import?",
    "answer": "No. Brevo documents the Brevo PushOwl app with customer, product and order sync plus onsite tracking and marketing automations."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can syncing overwrite subscription information?",
    "answer": "Yes. Brevo warns that syncing can override existing contact attributes, including subscription status; review consent settings before importing."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a Brevo contact import move Shopify receipts?",
    "answer": "Do not assume it does. Assign and verify the sender of each receipt and other transactional message separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should historical contacts enter recovery immediately?",
    "answer": "Use a fresh eligible checkout to validate enrollment; an imported customer or old order is not proof of live cart tracking."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Audit imported permission and transactional ownership before moving recovery.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Mailchimp", "reason": "Retain a working audience and store-connected recovery."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Brevo", "reason": "Verify account mapping, permission precedence and live events."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Make migration a data decision before a pricing decision</h2>
<p>Mailchimp is a reasonable starting point when an established audience and store-connected recovery already work. Evaluate Brevo when the team wants its marketing and transactional tooling and can name who will maintain the Shopify connection. A lower headline subscription is not sufficient if importing customers changes their permission state or an assumed receipt migration leaves a gap.</p>
<h2>Use the current Shopify connection</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo’s current Shopify guide describes Brevo PushOwl</a>: Shopify customer, product and order synchronization, an onsite tracker, and marketing automations including abandonment. It also warns that sync can override existing contact attributes, including subscription status. Plan this as an account and data mapping exercise, not a CSV upload followed by an immediate campaign.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/18720609955474-Migrate-to-the-Brevo-PushOwl-app-with-an-existing-Brevo-account">Brevo has a separate migration guide for an existing account</a>. Use it before connecting a store already associated with Brevo. Confirm which account receives events and which team can access it. A populated contact list in the wrong account will not validate your intended automation.</p>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s Shopify guide</a> warns that existing abandoned-cart automations need recreating and that welcome automations can create redundant welcomes when connecting. Preserve useful audience history while reviewing new-flow enrollment separately. Its <a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/create-abandoned-cart-customer-journey/">abandoned-cart flow documentation</a> supports store-connected recovery; the choice is not between broadcasts and automation.</p>
<h2>Agree on consent precedence before historical sync</h2>
<p><a href="https://docs.pushowl.com/en/articles/14660088-source-of-truth-for-marketing-consent">PushOwl’s consent guide</a> explains direction settings and that fresh historical sync into a new Brevo account loads Shopify consent. In an existing account, explicitly allowing Shopify subscription status to override Brevo has different consequences. Check the chosen source against your actual suppression records before starting the sync. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<p>For a proposed dry run, reconcile a subscribed customer, a Mailchimp-unsubscribed customer, an existing Brevo-unsubscribed customer, a guest buyer and an address with conflicting records. Record source, timestamp, channel, destination status and the resolution for each. Importing an address or order does not supply missing permission evidence. Preserve the more restrictive state until the conflict is resolved; do not let a historical import silently reactivate an unsubscribe.</p>
<h2>Separate marketing from transactional ownership</h2>
<p><a href="https://developers.brevo.com/docs/send-a-transactional-email">Brevo’s transactional API guide</a> describes sending action-triggered messages such as confirmations and password resets, with a configured sender. That capability is not evidence that installing a Shopify marketing app reroutes every Shopify notification. A receipt, shipping notice, account message and cart promotion each need an identified sender and trigger.</p>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Migration acceptance condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Contact synchronization</td><td>Known customer and permission records match the approved mapping.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery enrollment</td><td>A new eligible checkout generates the intended flow entry and working basket link.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchase exit</td><td>An order during the wait cancels pending promotional recovery.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transactional sending</td><td>The designated system sends one correct confirmation; another sender does not duplicate it.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>If the store keeps Shopify as receipt owner, verify that behavior while changing marketing. If a custom service uses Brevo for another transactional message, its owner must verify the event, template, delivery log and duplicate prevention independently. Sending a promotion through a transactional endpoint does not make the content a service notification.</p>
<h2>Proposed checkout and import acceptance exercise</h2>
<p>Keep the destination flow non-sending while checking a new subscriber’s checkout, a historical buyer’s new cart and a non-subscriber. Confirm which behavior reaches the account, then inspect eligibility and the planned send. Purchase before the delay expires, replay the purchase event, unsubscribe while waiting and check that a queued message is handled as intended. Record event IDs and timestamps; contact counts and old order totals alone cannot pass this exercise. These are proposed checks, not observed results.</p>
<p>For mobile or gift abandonment, verify the basket link, gift message and variant selection. For international shipping shock, clarify actual charges rather than applying a generic code. A subscription toggle needs the correct purchase type restored; a B2B MOQ block needs the threshold or sales route. If BNPL or a financing widget caused hesitation, explain available payment options without promising approval. Suppress fully unavailable baskets and check available variants before sending.</p>
<p>For a returning customer, inspect recent purchases and open support requests. Check cancellation and partial-refund events separately from ordinary purchase exits. A broken bundle offer needs its eligibility fixed; test welcome and cart coupon combinations so the imported audience does not receive an unintended stacked reduction. Add SMS only after verifying separate channel permission and send windows, with one coordinated contact cap.</p>
<h2>Model incentive and operating costs separately</h2>
<p>Hypothetical gross-profit examples with goods cost fixed: $367 at 46% margin gives $168.82 before other expenses; a 14% code costs $51.38 and leaves $117.44. A $292 basket at 38% gives $110.96; a 10% code costs $29.20 and leaves $81.76. Shipping, fees, returns, software and staff costs still have to be deducted. The former 114-minute/37-hour/7-hour ladder is not an experimentally established best schedule.</p>
<p>Price the actual eligible contact population, expected campaign and automation sends, channel charges and required features in each account. Add the time to reconcile imports and maintain any transactional integration. Use consistent order windows and count profit after discounts; a randomized holdout can estimate incremental orders if the sample is sufficient, while platform-attributed sales cannot establish that alone.</p>
<h2>Release and rollback</h2>
<p>Keep a pre-sync permission export and mapping decisions. Reconcile pending Mailchimp recovery messages, pause that flow, then enable the verified replacement for a limited eligible cohort. Stop the replacement if subscriptions change unexpectedly, a new checkout does not enroll or a receipt duplicates. Reconcile its queue before restoring the former recovery owner; preserve later unsubscribe changes rather than restoring an obsolete contact snapshot wholesale.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
